  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Uptick in heat and humidity for midweek.\n\nFog forming in the valleys of the northern and central mtns as\nof 06Z. Should be just as, in not more, extensive than Mon AM.\nThe fog should burn away early this morning in most places, but\ncould take until 10 AM in the deepest, darkest recesses of\nCentral PA.\n\nAbundant sunshine, mixed with some flat cumulus and high, thin \ncirrus clouds will kick off the shortened school and work week \nTuesday. As the center of this area high pressure drifts \nsoutheast of PA on today and Wednesday, return southwesterly \nflow will usher in an uptick and heat and humidity. \n\nThe upper level ridge centered over the Central US will also \nbegin to nudge into the Mid Atlantic while beginning to flatten.\n\nHighs will range from the upper 70s to mid 80s today. We'll add\nanother 1-3F on Wed. Combined with dewpoints back in the mid to\nupper 60s, heat index values may touch the 90F mark in the \nsouth central mountains on Wednesday. Rain-free conditions will\nprevail and we'll continue to enjoy a break from very active \nstretch of severe weather we've dealt with this summer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Showers, storms, and gusty winds Wednesday night\ninto Thursday.\n\nBy Wednesday night, a low pressure system will be moving across\nnorthern Ontario with a cold front extending south through the \nGreat Lakes and Ohio Valley region. The pressure gradient \nwill support an uptick in synoptic scale/gradient winds \nWednesday afternoon into Thursday. Expect gusts into the 20s on\nthe hilltops and also in the SE. A gust near 30MPH is possible\non the tops of the Laurels Wed night just before the front. \n\nThe cold front will spark SHRA/TSRA, with the majority occurring\nbefore the front passes later Wed night N and Thurs in the S. \nThe risk for severe weather will depend on the magnitude of a \nshortwave trough racing through the region and any associated \njet dynamics in play. \n\nVertical wind profiles look unidirectional, so the primary \nhazard would be from damaging winds. Timing of the cold front\nand dewpoints largely staying out of the upper 60s to lower\n70s range are the 2 main reasons to think storms will not get\ntoo crazy, unlike recent events. Timing of the cold front has\nbeen similar the last few days. SPC outlook currently has a MRGL\nrisk area for Wed-night painted across the NW half of the CWA.\nSeems in line with previous forecasts. The new Day3 (Thur-night)\noutlook barely has the general thunder area into the CWA - \nbasically along and S of the PA Turnpike. The current WPC \noutlook for Thurs paints a SLGT risk of excessive rainfall over\nthe SW quarter of PA - including the Laurels. However, it will\nbe difficult to squeeze out heavy rain without thunder/deep\nconvection. So, we'll chat with WPC about the threat risk. \n\nHigh pressure builds behind the cold front, giving way to a dry\nand sunny Friday. There could be a few PM SHRA/TSRA Saturday\nand/or Sunday afternoons, especially over the western mtns. A \nmore-active (wetter) pattern is in store for Mon-Tues. But, the\nflat upper ridge over the southern half of the CONUS will make \nit difficult for deep moisture to make it into PA ahead of any \nshots of atmos forcing. The second half of the week probably \nends dry as the upper ridge pumps up over the central CONUS.",
